d aspherical manifold.\n\nIts fundamental group is\n\nK⋊Ad γ 2 r+1​​Z≅G 2 r+1​.\n\nIt would therefore be a manifold model of the forbidden odd cover. This contradiction proves that no homeomorphism realizes δ. Although δ 2=1 as an outer class, the argument did not assume that the hypothetical homeomorphism has finite order. Excluding only involutory representatives would be weaker. (OpenAI, 2026)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHERIOT\nWATT\nUNIVERSITYHeriot-Watt University\nResea (https://researchportal.hw.ac.uk/files/25100063/HNN_extensions_of_triangle_groups.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; the presentation of the HNN-extension \\(H_{\\mathcal P}=H*_{(K_{\\mathcal P},\\phi)}\\) is aspherical, and so ... Condition (4) (the map \\(\\phi:a\\mapsto b,\\ b\\mapsto b^{-1}a^{-1}\\) is non-inner of order three).Note that non-equilateral triangle groups have outer automorphism groups of\nTo obtain the groups $T_p$ from Theorem A, take in Theorem 4.2 the group $H$ to be $T_i$, the automorphism $\\phi$ to be $a\\mapsto b$,\n$b\\mapsto b^{-1}a^{-1}$, and the malcharacteristic subgroup in Condition (3) to be the subgroup $M:=\\langle x,y\\rangle$ stated in Section 3, above.Theorem 7.9 is a related construction, and also follows from Theorem 4.2.\nIndeed, Theorem 4.2 proves Theorem A and Theorem 7.9 modulo proving that\nthe subgroup \\(M=\\langle x,y\\rangle\\) of \\(T_{i,j,k}\\), \\(i,j,k\\ge 6\\), is malcharacteristic and free of\nrank two; that is, modulo proving that Condition (3) of Theorem 4.2 holds\nfor these triangle groups. This is because the groups \\(T_{i,j,k}\\) are well-known\nto possess Conditions (1) and (2), while the groups \\(T_i\\) additionally possess\nCondition (4) (the map \\(\\phi:a\\mapsto b,\\ b\\mapsto b^{-1}a^{-1}\\) is non-inner of order three).\nNote that non-equilateral triangle groups have outer automorphism groups of\norder two or four (see Lemma 7.3), so Con